{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1509331-how-tribes-give-back-to-the-urdaga-community","id":1509331,"title":"How Tribes Give Back to the Urdaga Community","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-04-22T17:46:24-07:00","id":1509331,"title":"How Tribes Give Back to the Urdaga Community","content":"I\u2019ve <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1181906-how-do-you-make-tribes-feel-distinct-and-interesting?p=1182130#post1182130\">written before</a> about my own take on Renown and how to use them to think about your werewolf\u2019s character and a little bit about Tribal attitudes as a result. I\u2019ve come up with some more ideas that I wanted to spell out and share. (And it's kind of a better response to shkspr's question, too.)<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">My take on Renown</span><br /> Going to do a quick review and restatement of my take, to hopefully write things up better than I did last time, but mostly to try to help clarify and set up the new thing.<br /> <br /> I don\u2019t really have anything useful to add to Cunning. By the book, <b>Cunning</b> is about bending, subverting, or breaking rules in service of getting stuff done, and that\u2019s all I actually have for it, too.<br /> <br /> In the rules, <b>Glory</b> is what it says on the tin: doing the biggest, craziest stuff that people will sing about for years. What I try to get at is a more psychological dimension: remembering the past grounds your identity and arms you to not repeat mistakes. This helps explain why Glory is desirable in the context of a community, not just as a synonym for being famous.<br /> <br /> <b>Honor</b> usually gets summarized as doing the diplomatic thing, but what I wanted to add was a kind of neurotic desire to make things fit into the boxes you\u2019ve set out. You should lead, but you should follow. You belong in the Shadow; you belong in the Flesh. It\u2019s a pretty non-diplomatic impulse that helps lead to the kind of micromanagement you\u2019d expect to see, but when kept in check, can be a useful perspective.<br /> <br /> By the rules, <b>Purity</b> is about honoring the Oath, doing your ancestral duty, and that kind of thing. I added the idea of proof or truth: that a big way that a werewolf expresses Purity is by constantly testing their beliefs against reality: for the Rahu, this usually means establishing who\u2019s stronger in a fight; for the Meninna, this usually means daring interlopers into their territory and hunting them.<br /> <br /> <b>Wisdom</b>, by the rules, focuses on integrating the spirit with the flesh and this ends up being an esoteric field to explore. My addition focuses on some of the consequences, where a lot of the law of the Shadow comes down to a sense of fair exchange and the idea that everything must have a price. They take the abstract notion of balance as an aspiration and call it inevitable. Ithaeur therefore tend to keep payment on hand for expected transactions, whereas Bone Shadows will often look at the Shadow as a kind of massive stock market for human emotion. (Something, something, Max Gladstone's The Craft Sequence.)<br /> <br /> These aren\u2019t supposed to be unalloyed virtues: they\u2019re all a kind of hubris when taken too far. But the idea is to try to dig into the psychology behind why a character would care about a particular Renown and extrapolate from there how that impulse could be shared by others of the same Auspice or Tribe.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Giving Back</span><br /> What I\u2019d like to add now is the idea that established Tribal elders, or aspiring veterans who want to establish themselves, in a geography will want to gain respect by facilitating events.<br /> <br /> At a basic level, this seems pretty straightforward: members of a Tribe will get together, talk shop about their favored prey, swap lies, and so on. But what do they contribute, as a Tribe, back to the wider Urdaga community? Even here, it\u2019s natural that members of their own Tribe will be most interested in what is on offer, these things are intentionally shaped to be open to other Tribes to help all Urdaga.<br /> <br /> <b>Iron Masters</b> teach classes. Humans are complicated prey and they are impossibly multifarious for even other humans to get a real handle on them. Iron Masters have, as a Tribe, devoted to their lives to figuring out what these little creatures are capable of. These classes might be anything: how to blend in and hide your wolfishness; how to use a technology competently; how legal structures work and ways to exploit them; how the local gangs are control their turf; and so on. Specifics should of course be tailored to the local area; packs in Silicon Valley would benefit from knowing techspeak, whereas how to navigate and suffer through a subway ride is pretty pointless knowledge. This also lets the teachers build up a reputation as mentors and a network of students who feel grateful, which end up being useful as favors to be called in later.<br /> <br /> <b>Blood Talons</b> are the people you approach to find out about the past. If a territory used to belong to another pack, who have been dead and gone for ten years now, your best bet for finding out what happened is a Blood Talon. If you want to find the original boundaries of a territory, or who used to claim a piece of land, then the Talons probably remember. If there\u2019s a wolf in charge of the local museum or library, it\u2019s just as likely it\u2019s a Talon rather than a Shadow. Homer, standing in a public square reciting the deeds of the past, would have been a Blood Talon.<br /> <br /> A different way to tackle the <b>Blood Talons</b> is that they\u2019re the artists of the Urdaga. The local indie film scene might have a Suthar Anzuth who is known for creating incredible movies about coming-of-age or the transgender experience as a subtle bit of education for young wolves, or there maybe there\u2019s a Talon who\u2019s known for spraying murals commemorating local events and people, or a local singer whose lyrics warn about how to know when it\u2019s time to stop being nice and cut ties with so-called allies and rip-and-tear in what\u2019s <i>definitely</i> an entirely metaphorical fight.<br /> <br /> <b>Storm Lords</b> are usually trying to build a Protectorate, sure, but what else? Storm Lords have the best resources about how humans can integrate with packs, how humans can learn about the Shadow with (relative) safety, how humans can strengthen their own wills to protect themselves from the depredations of spirits. While the Farsil Luhal are the experts in exploiting humans and making them do what the pack needs, it\u2019s the Iminir who actually focus on protecting them from everything, who take the charge of shepherding humanity most seriously. They aren\u2019t going to advocate for human inclusion into packs, but if you\u2019re going to do it, they\u2019ll help you do it as well as can be done. If you can\u2019t ban it, you can at least regulate it.<br /> <br /> <b>Hunters in Darkness</b> tend to be quite possessive of their territory, but in this specific case, they proactively invite other packs in. What for? A tournament. While the Iminir talk a big game about everyone getting along and the Talons try to scare you into line with pointed threats, the Meninna throw out a public gauntlet and openly invite everyone to demonstrate who the top dog in the area is. The tournament is almost always invite-only to prevent non-Uratha, but in practice, anyone who can prove themselves in the ring is welcome. There are always strong rules in place, enforced by Shadow-binding rites, to make sure there aren\u2019t any deathmatches or high risk of Kuruth, but it\u2019s never perfectly clean when Uratha are involved. The point, ultimately, is a clear pecking order between packs or individuals, which makes it a lot easier to enforce the peace according to the Oath.<br /> <br /> <b>Bone Shadows</b> run a fucking market. It\u2019s less a bazaar of wonders, though, and more of an auction where packs or individuals will put their services for sale to the highest bidder. Spirits and occultists will find their way to these shows, and traveling packs often find them incredibly useful for demonstrating their value to a community in which they\u2019re interloping foreigners with no turf and unclear strength. They aren\u2019t common events by any means, but even more rarely, you\u2019ll have non-Urdaga stepping up. In between such shows, however, the Hirfathra Hissu are still good powerbrokers to talk to if you need to a proxy to do a job: they\u2019ll know a guy who knows a spirit who knows a guy. Their actual goal is to get people to reveal the price at which they can be bought.<br /> <br /> <br /> I generally look at these as ways for high-status Tribe members to show off why their Renown is the most important, and most valuable, of the five. It\u2019s a kind of flip-side to the \u201cmost dangerous prey\u201d dynamic. There should also be interesting ways to combine these projects with the kind of priorities of various Auspices.<br /> <br /> And I ended up doing two for the Blood Talons because I felt my first take was pretty weak. I think the masterful artist thing is better, and if you want to bring the Talons back to being fighty boys, you can take a wuxia angle and use the bodies of your enemies as the ink on your asphault canvas or something. That\u2019s one way to get Glory 5. Still not really a Tribe-level thing, though.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">How to Use</span><br /> Obviously, however you want. Mainly, I think half of them are useful as story hooks. You can kick an arc off with the catalyst being something going wrong at a Meninna tournament, or a visiting pack announcing themselves at a Bone Shadow auction. Having an Iminir or Farsil Luhal running seminars at the community center might be a venue for the player pack to initially meet for the first time, and regular attendance off-screen is a good excuse to justify new Merits. These are also decent ideas if a character doesn\u2019t know what else to do for their last Renown dot: organize a class, arrange an auction, plan out a tournament, and it can be a big thing to cement their local reputation as a community leader.<br /> <br /> Differently, they\u2019re different ways of pitching characters. A community historian or graffiti artist isn\u2019t an obvious job for a Blood Talon, but it makes complete sense in my framework, and it may mean you can position NPCs around town differently. And letting Storm Lords be just as spiritual as Bone Shadows, but in a much more introverted sense, also makes it more reasonable for Bone Shadows to be gregarious and slimy as often as they are mysterious and creepy.<br /> <br /> Hell, you could even do something as simple as having your character play substitute teacher for an Iron Master class while the player is unavailable for a session, and it's a convenient way to do off-screen things like having an NPC pass them a tip they can come into the next session with.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, I think stuff like the market and the tournament can be markers of maturity for an Urdaga community. They might be good for players who are looking for non-idigam final challenges, or as a kind of this-place-has-its-shit-together thing when the players are newcomers in town. But they also might in turn be signs of decadence, where the shindig is taking priority over more basic duties, and the local Predator Kings are taking the events as a party-crash invite.<br /> <br /> <br /> As an end note, one thing I'm realizing is very lacking from this write-up is the notion of savagery and fury. I've been reading a book on peacemaking lately and it's been making me think about Werewolf somehow, but not in a rage-monster sense. A lot of that probably bled into this, so take that for what it's worth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-22T20:22:40-07:00","id":1509345,"title":"","content":"I like your ideas.<br /> <br /> I think for blood talons I would be inclined to say they're the FBI. They hunt the worst of the oath breakers, but also keep the other tribes vigilant about policing their own, because if they don't they will draw even more blood talon attention. Basically they inspire the other tribes to be better in that way. <br /> <br /> The Iron Masters can kind of be fire fighters in a related scenario, they can put out political fires among the humans the other tribes cannot. They can help keep the blood suckers, wizards, and everyone else off the tribes backs as well. <br /> <br /> The Hunters in Darkness are the bogey men to the foes of the Uratha. You might be big and bad enough to march over and take the Blood Talon pack in the town over, or the Storm Lord's a couple miles to the north, but are you big and bad enough to take a pack of Hunters who strike you from the back on the way there that you never realized were there? I guess they're kind of the National Guard in that way. <br /> <br /> The Bone Shadows are the masters of an entirely different world. They have the wisdom of the past, they have the wisdom of another world, they are not just a defense against that world, but a bridge a bridge to the good things within the Hisil, they are the bridge to the past and the shadow marshes. <br /> <br /> The Storm Lords inspire others to be strong by showing their strength. They're the athletes, movie stars, and other professional role models that the Uratha can look up to. But it isn't just their not showing weakness that puts them into that role, they're proactive in the way the other tribes can't be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","name":"ShadowcatX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27618_2.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-24T19:57:08-07:00","id":1509548,"title":"","content":"Definitely a cool idea to give some sinew and gristle to werewolf society. I also like how the majority of your tribal roles avoid easy stereotypes by digging in deeper to the philosophy of Renown.<br /> <br /> I have a suggestion for a second avenue for Hunters in Darkness, similar to your dual-approached Blood Talons: The Meninna also work as contracted territory managers. If your pack's recently suffered significant losses or the hunt's taking you far away for a while, you can rest easy that, for the right favors traded, the Hunters will have your back, shoring up the weak spots in your territory's infrastructure and defense. The flip-side to this is, due to their focus on Purity, if a pack is obviously fucking up their duties bad enough (and especially if said fuck-ups result in increased Host activity), the Meninna <i>will</i> step in and take charge if they think they can get away with it. Just a thought. Anyway nice work!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-01T06:51:08-07:00","id":1510348,"title":"","content":"Ooh, nice. This feels like giving the Bone anchor trait to the Tribes, whereas their standard portrayals in the books would be their Blood anchor trait.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.t